# SYNOPSIS

```text
echo start gpu-test >/svc/launcher/ctl
```

# DESCRIPTION

gpu-test is an app of the board images: it runs only when the launcher is asked to start it (launcher(8)), and prints its lines to the log.
It is a client of the GPU class's `csf` capset (gpu-csf-context(5)): the first GPU under `/dev` (`/dev/gpu<n>` or `/dev/display<n>`, its `gpu` service) that offers the capset.
Where none does, as in QEMU, it first says why (what `/dev` holds, and for each `display` or `gpu` entry whether its `gpu` node answers and which capsets it offers), then prints `gpu-test: skipped: no csf GPU` and exits 0.

It makes a context with a VM, a buffer of two pages bound at an address of its own, a group of one queue and a sync, and runs three things on it.
First, a stream it wrote itself: MOV48 and STORE_STATE of the GPU's timestamp into the buffer, then MOV48 and SYNC_ADD64 of 1 into a counter word of the buffer.
It submits it with its sync as the signal and waits up to 10 seconds for the counter to reach 1, then reads the buffer back: the timestamp is not 0, the counter word is 1, and the group's state is clean.
The check prints `gpu-test: ok: the GPU ran a command stream` and the timestamp.
Second, a stream whose CALL goes to an address nothing maps.
The GPU's MMU faults, the group ends, and the sync is raised all the same.
The group's state has the fatal fault bit and names its queue, and a further submit on the context fails with `lost`.
Then a fresh context makes the first stream again, and it runs.
The check prints `gpu-test: ok: a faulting stream ended only its group`.
Third, a hundred jobs with no stream, each submitted with a signal and waited for: the median of the time from the submit to the counter's rise, as `budget name=gpu.mali.empty_job_us value=<us> unit=us`.

Each check that fails prints `gpu-test: FAILED:` and what failed, and the program exits 1.
Its last line is `gpu-test: passed`.

